description:
Context: V6__device_metadata.sql added devices.device_type and backfilled it heuristically:
  UPDATE ... SET device_type='ERV' WHERE device_type IS NULL AND lower(device_name) LIKE 'erv%';
  UPDATE ... SET device_type='FCU' WHERE device_type IS NULL AND ae200_device_id IS NOT NULL AND lower(device_name) NOT LIKE 'erv%';

This produced the correct 10 FCU + 2 ERV in prod today (verified). But it is a point-in-time guess with no constraint enforcing it going forward.

Problem: app/db.py is_erv_device()/is_fcu_device() and the rewritten rules engine (run_rules_for_device branches on device.erv) trust device_type. A device added later with a NULL device_type silently won't match device.erv / is_fcu_device, so rules will not act on it -- with no error. This is the same class of trap as the ERV generalization in the rules rewrite: behavior keyed on metadata that nothing guarantees is set.

Fix direction: ensure the device-creation/ingest path always sets device_type, and/or add a startup/health check (or test) that flags controllable devices (ae200_device_id IS NOT NULL) with NULL device_type.
